{"step_index":192,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:20:57Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-07T07:20:57+01:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-07T07:21:04+01:00\nThe search for \"github \"StevenBlack/hosts\" ad blocker python proxy \"CONNECT\"\" returned the following summary:\nIf you are looking to build or implement a **Python-based HTTP/HTTPS proxy server** that acts as an ad blocker by leveraging the well-known blocklists from **`StevenBlack/hosts`**[1][2] (which map unwanted tracking, malware, and ad domains to `0.0.0.0` or `127.0.0.1`)[3][4], handling the **`CONNECT`** method is a crucial requirement[5].\n\nUnlike plain HTTP requests (where the proxy sees the full URL like `http://example.com/ad.js`), HTTPS traffic requires an HTTP **`CONNECT`** tunnel[6][5]. When a client wants to load a secure site, it first sends a `CONNECT` command to the proxy (e.g., `CONNECT ad.doubleclick.net:443 HTTP/1.1`), asking the proxy to open a TCP tunnel to the destination.\n\nBelow is an overview of how a Python proxy processes `CONNECT` requests alongside a `StevenBlack`-style hosts blocklist.\n\n---\n\n### Conceptual Architecture of a Hosts-Based Ad-Blocking Proxy\n\n1. **Load the Blocklist:** Download or parse the raw text file from `StevenBlack/hosts` (e.g., the unified hosts file)[7][8] into a fast Python lookup structure (like a `set` for $O(1)$ complexity).\n2. **Start a TCP Proxy Server:** Listen on a local port (e.g., `127.0.0.1:8080`)[9].\n3. **Intercept Client Requests:** \n   * **For standard HTTP:** Read the request line, extract the hostname, and check it against the blocklist.\n   * **For HTTPS (`CONNECT`):** Parse the target host and port out of the `CONNECT` header. Check the hostname against the blocklist *before* opening any tunnel.\n4. **Action:**\n   * **If Blocked:** Drop the connection, return an HTTP `403 Forbidden` (or a `502/504` error code), or reply with a connection close so ads/trackers fail to load silently.\n   * **If Allowed:** Establish a socket connection to the real destination and relay the TCP stream transparently.\n\n---\n\n### Minimal Skeleton: Python Proxy Handling `CONNECT` & Domain Filtering\n\nThis conceptual example demonstrates how a custom threading/async Python proxy catches the `CONNECT` method and checks it against a blocked domains list derived from sources like `StevenBlack/hosts`:\n\n```python\nimport socket\nimport threading\n\n# Simulated StevenBlack blocklist set for demonstration\n# In production, parse lines from StevenBlack's raw hosts file:\n# e.g., if line starts with '0.0.0.0' or '127.0.0.1', add domain to BLOCKED_DOMAINS\nBLOCKED_DOMAINS = {\n    \"ad.doubleclick.net\",\n    \"trackers.example-ads.com\",\n    \"analytics.malware-domain.test\"\n}\n\ndef is_blocked(host):\n    # Strip port if present (e.g., \"ad.doubleclick.net:443\" -> \"ad.doubleclick.net\")\n    hostname = host.split(\":\")[0].strip().lower()\n    return hostname in BLOCKED_DOMAINS\n\ndef handle_client(client_socket):\n    try:\n        request_data = client_socket.recv(8192)\n        if not request_data:\n            return\n\n        first_line = request_data.decode('utf-8', errors='ignore').split('\\n')[0]\n        parts = first_line.split()\n        if len(parts) < 2:\n            client_socket.close()\n            return\n\n        method, target = parts[0], parts[1]\n\n        # Handle HTTPS tunneling via CONNECT\n        if method == \"CONNECT\":\n            host_port = target   antiphonal target like \"ads.yahoo.com:443\"\n            \n            if is_blocked(host_port):\n                # Blocked: Send HTTP 403 or drop connection\n                client_socket.sendall(b\"HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden\\r\\n\\r\\n\")\n                client_socket.close()\n                return\n\n            # Allowed: Connect to the remote server and establish the tunnel\n            target_host, target_port = host_port.split(\":\")\n            remote_socket = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n            \n            try:\n                remote_socket.connect((target_host, int(target_port)))\n            except Exception:\n                client_socket.sendall(b\"HTTP/1.1 502 Bad Gateway\\r\\n\\r\\n\")\n                client_socket.close()\n                return\n\n            # Notify client that the tunnel is established\n            client_socket.sendall(b\"HTTP/1.1 200 Connection Established\\r\\n\\r\\n\")\n\n            # Start bi-directional relay between client and remote destination\n            _tunnel(client_socket, remote_socket)\n\n        else:\n            # Handle standard HTTP proxy requests here if needed\n            client_socket.close()\n\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error handling request: {e}\")\n        client_socket.close()\n\ndef _tunnel(client, remote):\n    sockets = [client, remote]\n    try:\n        while True:\n            # Simple select-based TCP packet forwarding loop\n            # (Omitted full select implementation for brevity)\n            pass\n    except Exception:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        client.close()\n        remote.close()\n\ndef run_proxy(host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8080):\n    server = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)\n    server.setsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_REUSEADDR, 1)\n    server.bind((host, port))\n    server.listen(100)\n    print(f\"[*] Proxy server running on {host}:{port}\")\n\n    while True:\n        client_sock, addr = server.accept()\n        client_thread = threading.Thread(target=handle_client, args=(client_sock,))\n        client_thread.daemon = True\n        client_thread.start()\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    run_proxy()\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Technical Challenges with `CONNECT` Ad-Blocking Proxies\n\n1. **HTTPS Content Visibility (MITM):** \n   When handling an encrypted `CONNECT` tunnel, a standard proxy only sees the destination domain name (e.g., `ads.example.com`) during the initial handshake. It *cannot* inspect the individual paths or file names inside that traffic (such as `https://ads.example.com/banner.png`) unless you implement a **Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) proxy** that generates dynamic SSL/TLS certificates signed by a local Root Certificate Authority trusted by your machine[10]. \n2. **Alternative Ready-Made Tools:**\n   Writing an asynchronous `CONNECT`-aware proxy from scratch can be tedious. If you want to use StevenBlack's lists within a robust proxy ecosystem, consider:\n   * **Privoxy:** A non-caching web proxy with advanced filtering capabilities. Projects like `adhosts2privoxy` exist specifically to convert `StevenBlack/hosts` files into Privoxy-compatible action/filter formats[11].\n   * **mitmproxy (Python):** A powerful Python-scriptable MITM proxy where you can write custom request-interception scripts that load blocklists natively and intercept/kill flows matching unwanted hosts[10].\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGaRmaFVqA2h5p4oTjK1nxWoogdccNEfaRMd8Na5U1AWZsRTqpIOOeEFfXVczNOSxomMM33PcqF8yLP5W6_03Xl1-muRcIhPfY4p7eX-ljgKI6vA9zddZHVSA==)\n[2] [awesome-privacy.xyz](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQERSRpoX5oFEoigFcEh7KIlKyyTi5FDHy9SGQxYONE8_29AmZzIyow_2HnnNx9WYkJkiA8WNm1BYQkCOLadxjIyfWaoUL777kp0CZRFi7saif3DSn6B89VUy-rru5yChI5I9JAldWgksQXPFcPehDdF3StUFULdMrpDkvGweBBXDlrr)\n[3] [context7.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFlojMlGGBAJUAYzZcUGbFQ7yOC9nBitkAyfbzB9YkELZ8zNftabibip8avRJvWWE91ORUOAqpnjxBEsylkZGVNVwcn0UqUI4RVqc9dwAXiVEecZGfcYgHDPC8q)\n[4] [jamieweb.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQETSkFQXQ9l5wgKZpUF0MqtxfC-CBbQir4qmec4hTvSO09dCVCyfYaTMTGBCnAplaCZOFUOz_oHAlhb5-T9fNPsalD7kMld2lu7KX2wF8Cqu-0GR4bqJSVL8gwUbJ3s5TldoXmAlFd_Fg==)\n[5] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH9O7e2F1PWaKMtsZyMFxPaaW7x4PovYU14n-92Fx